# {{.Service}}

API (HTTP) service on CloudWeGo Hertz, generated by devkit.

```sh
make tools        # once per machine: hz + kitex + thriftgo
make gen          # regenerate the code that comes from the IDLs listed in idl.mk, and the OpenAPI document; locks the framework's files read-only afterwards
make run          # conf/local.yaml: your machine, with the Nacos on it (the file says how to start one)
make lint         # devkit lint; the pre-push hook runs it with make test before every push
make test         # one test: make test RUN=TestXxx
make verify       # the post-deploy checks (app/verify.go plus the framework's) against conf/local.yaml
curl 'http://127.0.0.1:{{.Port}}/ping?message=hi'
```

- The HTTP API is defined in the project's shared IDL repository, as a
  directory: `../idl/{{.Service}}/<domain>.thrift`, **one file per domain, one
  handler package per domain** (`namespace go <domain>`, routes
  `/v1/<domain>/...`, implemented in `handler/<domain>/`); `docs.thrift` holds
  only the documentation page's title and groups. A method is a route, its
  `api.post="/v1/<domain>/<verb>"` annotation says which (the convention is POST
  with a JSON body). Change the IDL there, run `make gen`, fill in the new
  function in `handler/<domain>/`, and push the IDL before the service. A new
  domain is a new file.
- A method returns its data struct (`common.Empty` when there is none); the
  handler ends with `hertzx.OK(c, &data)` or `hertzx.Fail(ctx, c, err)` and the
  framework adds the `{code, msg, data}` envelope. Request validation is
  `hertzx.Bind(c, &req)` (hz's stub says `c.BindAndValidate`; replace it).
  Business codes come from `../idl/errors/*.thrift`.
- The API documentation is generated from the IDL: `make gen` writes
  `cmd/{{.Service}}/openapi.yaml` and the service serves it at `/docs`
  (interactive, "Send request") and `/openapi.yaml` when `docs.enabled` is on
  (local, dev). Comments on methods and fields in the IDL are the descriptions;
  front ends generate their types and error-code tables from `/openapi.yaml`.
- What `make gen` writes: `hertz_gen/` (request and response structs),
  `router/` (the routes) and `kitex_gen/` (clients of the RPC services and the
  error codes). None of it is committed; CI regenerates it. The exceptions are
  yours and are committed: `handler/<domain>/` (hz adds an empty function for a
  method that is new and never touches the others) and `router/*/middleware.go`
  (middleware per route or group).
- To call an RPC service, add its IDL to `IDLS` in `idl.mk` (for example
  `ser-order/ser-order.thrift`), run `make gen`, declare the client in
  `deps/deps.go` and create it in `app/app.go`; the comment on `Setup` shows
  how. A handler uses it as `deps.Order` and passes the `ctx` of the request
  on: it carries the trace_id and the caller's identity to the RPC service.
  (Not `app.Order`: a Hertz handler already has an `app`, the package of
  `*app.RequestContext`.)
- Scheduled jobs go in `app/jobs.go`: a name, a cron schedule (UTC) and a
  function. `make jobs` lists them, `make job NAME=<name>` runs one here. The
  deployment turns the list into one Kubernetes CronJob per job; a run is a
  root trace and a log with its `run_id`, locked against overlap and cancelled
  at its timeout.
- What is yours and what is the framework's: `app/` (`app.go` wiring,
  `jobs.go`, `verify.go`), `deps/`, `handler/`, `router/*/middleware.go`,
  `conf/*.yaml`, `idl.mk`, `go.mod` and this file are yours and never
  rewritten. `cmd/{{.Service}}/main.go`, the Makefile, `.hz`, the CI files, the
  Dockerfile, `conf/README*.md`, `hertz_gen/`, `kitex_gen/`, the generated
  `router/` files, `cmd/*/openapi.yaml`, `.idl/`, `idl.lock` and `.devkit/`
  belong to the framework: `devkit update` replaces them and `devkit lock`
  keeps them read-only on your machine.
- Configuration is `conf/<APP_ENV>.yaml`; `${VAR}` placeholders are read from
  the environment. Four environments: `local` (no `APP_ENV`: your own machine,
  registered in the Nacos on it), `dev` (the shared development server), `uat`
  and `prod`. To work from your machine against the development server's Nacos,
  see `register: false` in `conf/local.yaml`; a laptop never registers there.
- The rules: how to add an endpoint or a domain, paging, error codes, lint and
  post-deploy checks are in the IDL repository's `AGENTS.md`; the interface
  conventions in its `README.md`; every framework capability in
  `{{.CommonModule}}` (checked out read-only at `../kit-common/`, see its
  `README.zh-CN.md`).
